description:
List, read, create, change, remove and run this project's agentic workflows. They live in .agents/workflows, which every folder of the project shares through Git; `hosts:` picks the machines that run each. A workflow is a prompt that runs itself when something happens — on a schedule, or when an agent finishes, asks for permission, raises a form, or stops, or on any event below.

A workflow is a Markdown file with YAML front matter. The front matter says what makes it run (`on:`) and which agent runs it (`agent:` — `new`, `standing`, or `triggering`); everything under the front matter is the prompt, sent verbatim.

A workflow meant only to be run by hand, with Run now on its page, says `on: manual` (or leaves `on:` out). Nothing else runs it.

A workflow may also say how its agent runs: `permission-mode:` (the runtime's own mode, e.g. a read-only or plan mode), `runtime:`, `model:`, `effort:` (how hard it thinks, e.g. `low` or `high`), and under `options:` any other option the runtime offers, by its own id — for example `fast: true` for fast mode. Leave them out and it runs on the default runtime with that runtime's own defaults. Set `permission-mode:` when the person says the workflow must not change anything — a workflow runs unattended, so this is the only chance to say so. A value the runtime does not offer stops the workflow running rather than falling back.

`cooldown:` (e.g. `15m`, `2h`, `1d`) is the least time from the start of one run to the start of the next. Triggers that arrive sooner, or while a run is going, are held and run once, with the latest of them, when it ends. Set one for a workflow on a busy event such as `agent.finished`.

`hosts:` names the computers that run it, by machine id: a Mac's hardware UUID, or on Linux the contents of `/etc/machine-id`. Leave it out and every host that has the folder runs it and lists it. Name ids and only those hosts run it and list it, on a schedule, on an event, or by hand. The id is the computer's, not its name. The workflow page writes it from the hosts the control plane knows; do not invent an id.

`when-done:` says what a run may do with its session once it is done: `keep` (the default) keeps every run in the list; `archive-allowed` lets a run that finishes done or nothing_to_do archive itself; `archive` archives every run that finishes so. Leave it out unless the person asks for runs to be put away.

Once its runtime has been used in this folder, reading a workflow also lists what that runtime offers for each of these, in the words the file takes. To change how an existing workflow runs, read it, then write it back whole with the settings changed.

Nothing here asks the person, but a workflow you write or change does not run until they approve it on the folder page; removing one takes effect at once. A new workflow you write also starts turned off, whatever its file says: after approving it, the person turns it on from its page when they are ready. Changing one that exists leaves it on or off as it was. So write one only when they asked for it, and say in your reply what you set up, that it is waiting for their OK, and that they turn it on once approved.

There is no limit on workflows waiting for approval. Up to 20 approved workflows may be on in each folder by default; the person can set the limit from 1 to 100 in Settings ▸ Cost. Turning one on past the limit is refused until you turn one off or archive one in that folder.

`run` runs a workflow once, now, as Run now on its page does: it starts the agent the workflow says and tells you its id, or says why it did not run. It is refused while the workflow waits for the person's OK, is archived or turned off, has a run going, or says `agent: triggering`; and in a run three workflows deep, so workflows cannot run each other in a loop. Run one only when the person asked for it or it is plainly the next step.

`disable` turns a workflow off without touching its file: it stays listed, marked off, and none of its triggers run it; `list` says which are off and why. `enable` turns one back on, but only one an agent turned off: one the person turned off, one an agent wrote and nobody has turned on yet, and one whose file says `enabled: false` are theirs to turn on.

Under on:, besides schedule, any event name works, narrowed by what its inputSchema below takes written under it, e.g. `- branch.moved:` with `branch: main` under it. The folder's MCP servers' events are listed by wait_for_event with action list.
Events (the same names work in wait_for_event and as workflow triggers under on:), one per line as MCP events/list describes them, each with its source: app, or the MCP server that offers it.
inputSchema is what where (or the keys under a trigger) may narrow it by; payloadSchema is the details it carries when it happens, as a woken agent is told them.
